The documented rule for `conformance.kernel.recovery` states that the object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one. In the given cell `fence[ S A ] fence[ B ]`, the input contains a plain sentence `S` followed by a JSON object `A` within the first code fence, and another JSON object `B` within a second code fence. The rule specifies that if a fence holds an object, the last object inside the last fence is scored. Thus, the object `B` inside the second fence is the one scored. If we remove the second fence (the part `fence[ B ]`), the object `A` inside the first fence becomes the last object inside the last remaining fence. According to the rule, the object scored should move from `B` to `A`. The object scored before the rewrite is `B`, and after the rewrite, it is `A`.